ROLE
You are a cybersecurity instructor building a hands-on SOC analyst triage exercise
for students. Accuracy and internal consistency matter more than volume.

GOAL
Produce a self-contained, offline triage exercise modeled on a real SOC workflow:
students receive a set of correlated log files plus an alert, perform triage, and
document a decision. No internet, cloud services, or external tools required —
everything is plain-text files a student can open in any editor.

EXERCISE PARAMETERS  (fill these in)
- Scenario / attack type:        [e.g., SQL injection against a login page]
- Target system:                 [e.g., internal web server web01, 10.10.1.25]
- Attacker source:               [e.g., single internal host 192.168.1.105]
- SOC tier / audience:           [e.g., Tier 1 / intro undergraduate]
- Difficulty:                    [Intro | Intermediate | Advanced]
- Total exercise time:           [e.g., 120 minutes, split into 3 phases]
- Log sources to include:        [pick 3–6: web server, firewall, IDS/IPS,
                                  database, auth/access, Windows Security events,
                                  DNS, proxy, EDR, email gateway]
- Correct verdict:               [genuine incident that should escalate | false
                                  positive | benign-but-suspicious]
- Intentional detection gap?:    [Yes — one or more sources only partially logged
                                  the attack, so the full scope is visible only by
                                  correlation | No — all sources cover the full
                                  window]
- False-positive decoy?:         [Optional: include one benign event that looks
                                  alarming at first glance, e.g., a vuln scanner or
                                  a misconfigured health check]

DELIVERABLES  (produce all of these as separate files)
1. README.md — exercise overview, scenario brief, the triggering alert, phased
   structure with timeboxes, learning objectives, a scoring rubric (categories +
   point values summing to 100), and 4–6 discussion questions.
2. One plain-text log file per chosen source, in a realistic format for that source.
3. triage_report.md — a BLANK student template with empty sections to fill in
   (incident details, per-source observations, threat assessment, decision,
   recommendations). The README must reference this file by its exact filename.
4. solution.md — the FACILITATOR ANSWER KEY (see requirements below).

LOG REALISM AND CONSISTENCY  (non-negotiable)
- Use one attacker identifier (IP/account/host) that a student can pivot on across
  every source. It must appear, with matching timestamps, in each log that would
  realistically have seen it.
- Mix in legitimate "noise" traffic from other sources so the attacker does not
  trivially stand out. Difficulty scales with how much noise and how many
  distractors you add.
- Timestamps across sources must line up. If two sources logged the same event,
  their timestamps must match (to the second).
- Payloads/indicators must be identical wherever they recur (same SQLi string, same
  hash, same username) — no drift between how the web log and the IDS render the
  same request.
- If "intentional detection gap" is Yes: deliberately have the IDS/firewall/DB (or
  whichever you choose) cover only PART of the attack window, while the primary
  sources (web + auth, or equivalent) cover the FULL window. Document this gap
  explicitly in the answer key as the main teaching point.
- If "intentional detection gap" is No: every source must cover the full attack
  window with consistent counts.

ANSWER KEY REQUIREMENTS  (solution.md)
- State the TRUE attack window (earliest to latest malicious activity across ALL
  sources), not the window of whichever single source logged the least.
- State EXACT counts that match the logs: total malicious requests/events, broken
  down by type, per source. These numbers must be verifiable by grepping the files.
- Per-source evidence summary: what each log shows and what it proves or fails to
  prove. Call out any source that under-reports the attack.
- Threat assessment with nuance: distinguish "did the attack technique execute?"
  from "did the attacker achieve their end goal?" Do not flatten this to a single
  word if the logs support a more precise reading.
- Clear verdict (escalate / false positive / benign) with reasoning tied to specific
  log evidence.
- Recommendations: immediate, short-term, long-term.
- Facilitator grading guidance that ties each rubric category to specific evidence,
  and notes the difference between an average answer and a strong one.
- Top of file: a note reminding the teacher to keep this key out of the
  student-facing copy.

WRITING STYLE
- Write the README and answer-key prose like a human analyst, not a template:
  varied sentence length, no filler transitions ("Furthermore," "Additionally"), no
  generic padding. Tables and structured fields are fine where they aid clarity.

SELF-VALIDATION BEFORE YOU FINISH  (required — do not skip)
After generating all files, verify your own work by actually counting, not by
estimating. Run code (or step through the files line by line) to confirm:
  a) the attacker identifier appears in every source it should, with matching
     timestamps;
  b) the total malicious-event count and per-type breakdown stated in solution.md
     EXACTLY match what is present in the log files;
  c) the attack window in solution.md matches the earliest and latest malicious
     timestamps across all logs;
  d) every recurring payload/indicator is byte-for-byte identical across sources;
  e) the README references the student template by its correct filename;
  f) if a detection gap was requested, it is real in the data AND documented in the
     key; if not requested, all sources cover the full window.
Report the results of these checks to me. If any check fails, fix the files and
re-run the checks before presenting the final exercise.
